From b721e7d83c316533d27510d0ef606ec90dcbf52e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 12:16:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kk: bound the device's tables of compiled Metal objects

The device's tables of compiled MSL libraries (0050) and Metal pipeline
states (0052) only grew: every library kept a strdup of its whole MSL
source as the key plus the retained MTLLibrary, and every pipeline state
ever built stayed in the table after the application destroyed its
pipeline. A title that creates and destroys pipelines for hours grew the
process without bound.

Both are now instances of one bounded table (struct kk_metal_cache):
entries are kept in use order and the least recently used ones past
kk_metal_cache::max (4096 per table, MESA_KK_METAL_CACHE_MAX) are
dropped, releasing the table's reference; a pipeline still using the
object holds its own. Libraries are keyed by the blake3 digest of their
source instead of the source, and the pipeline keys name their libraries
by the same digests rather than by address, since a library may have been
dropped and another compiled at its address. The single-flight behaviour
is unchanged: an entry without an object is a compile in flight that
other callers wait for, and a failed compile drops the entry (its key is
freed by the table's key destructor). Publishing requires the reservation's
exact key pointer as well as an empty object: if allocation in find failed,
another thread may have reserved an equal key while this caller compiled.
It must not publish into that thread's reservation or free its live key.

kk_CreateDevice sets up the three tables in one step
(kk_metal_caches_init) before the compile pool and returns
VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY when it fails, tearing down only what was
initialised, preserving 0050's failure cleanup; a missing Metal compiler is
VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED.

Generated-by: LLM
